Wire Free PiZZa enter_suspend_mode() into rpi-open-firmware _main()

Only ONE hook is safe to install at this stage:
  - enter_suspend_mode() replaces monitor_start(), so after platform
    init the VPU enters suspend-to-RAM instead of dropping into the
    IPC monitor (which would spin the ARM chainloader and try to boot
    Linux).

The paired check_resume_from_suspend() call is DELIBERATELY NOT
installed. That hook reads SDRAM at 0x1FF00000 BEFORE sdram_init()
runs, which the vc4-suspend author documented as risky on cold boot
("if SDRAM lost power, reads return garbage and magic won't match")
but never tested on silicon. On our Zero 2 W it hangs the AXI bus at
that pre-sdram_init read: total UART silence, meter reads ~30 mA.
Cold boot cannot rely on a magic-number probe of SDRAM — the SDRAM
controller isn't up yet.

The include itself is retained: it declares the API (both hooks) but
is otherwise harmless.

Applied against pristine librerpi/rpi-open-firmware submodule 2725d94.

--- a/firmware/romstage.c
+++ b/firmware/romstage.c
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 #include "interrupt.h"
 #include "traps.h"
 #include "otp.h"
+#include "../../src/boot_suspend_hook.h"
 
 uint32_t g_CPUID;
 
@@ -136,8 +137,8 @@
   PEStartPlatform(&otpInfo);
   __asm__ volatile("ei");
 
-	/* start vpu monitor */
-	monitor_start();
+	/* Free PiZZa: trigger suspend-to-RAM instead of the VPU monitor. */
+	enter_suspend_mode();
 
 	panic("main exiting!");
 }
